%I A004129 M2525 #79 Aug 09 2026 15:55:11
%S A004129 1,3,6,9,13,17,22,27,33,40,47,56,65,74,83,94,105,117,129
%N A004129 Postage stamp problem: largest m such that there exists an n-subset S of nonnegative integers such that 1,...,m can be expressed as a sum of two distinct elements of S.
%C A004129 From _Ed Pegg Jr_, Aug 03 2026: (Start)
%C A004129 Stamp solutions appear in gap families, similar to sparse rulers (A326499).
%C A004129 a(20) = #{0, 1, 2, 3, 6, 10, 14, 18, 22, 26, 30, 34, 37, 38, 39, 40, 80, 83, 86, 89},
%C A004129   with range 129 and gaps {1, 1, 1, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 4, 3, 1, 1, 1, 40, 3, 3, 3},
%C A004129   leading to TreskillingYellow[4,7] = 1^3, 3, 4^7, 3, 1^3, 40, 3^3. Some stamp families:
%C A004129   BlueMauritius[t,r] = 1^t, t, (t+1)^r, t, 1^t
%C A004129   Cottonreel[t,r] = 1^2, 2^t, 1^2, 2t+4, 3, (2t+5,3)^r
%C A004129   PennyBlack[t,r] = 1^(t-8), 2, 4, 1, t-6, 2, 1^2, t-1, t^r
%C A004129   TreskillingYellow[t,r] = 1^(t-1), t-1, t^r, t-1, 1^(t-1), tr+4(t-1), (t-1)^(t-1)
%C A004129   Magenta[t,r] = 1^t, t^r, 1^t, tr, (t+1)^(t-1), t(r+2)+1, (t+1)^(t-1)
%C A004129 Magenta (see Kohonen ex.3) ties TreskillingYellow at 222 stamps and wins thereafter.
%C A004129 Next terms if TY wins: 141, 154, 169, 184, 199, 214, 229, 247, 265, 283, 301. (End)
%D A004129 N. J. A. Sloane and Simon Plouffe, The Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1995 (includes this sequence).
%H A004129 R. L. Graham and N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="http://neilsloane.com/doc/RLG/073.pdf">On Additive Bases and Harmonious Graphs</a>, SIAM J. Algebraic and Discrete Methods, 1 (1980), 382-404, doi:10.1137/0601045.
%H A004129 J. Kohonen, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1606.04770">An improved lower bound for finite additive 2-bases</a>, arXiv:1606.04770 [math.NT], 2016-2017.
%H A004129 Ed Pegg Jr, <a href="/A004129/a004129.txt">List of A004129 solutions</a>.
%H A004129 Simon Plouffe, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/0911.4975">Approximations de séries génératrices et quelques conjectures</a>, Dissertation, Université du Québec à Montréal, 1992; arXiv:0911.4975 [math.NT], 2009.
%H A004129 Simon Plouffe, <a href="/A000051/a000051_2.pdf">1031 Generating Functions</a>, Appendix to Thesis, Montreal, 1992.
%H A004129 <a href="/index/Pos#postage_stamp_problem">Index to sequences related to the postage stamp problem</a>
%F A004129 The g.f. (z^4+z^3+2*z^2+2*z+1)*(z^2+z+1)/((z-1)*(z^5+z^4+z^3-z-1)) conjectured by _Simon Plouffe_ in his 1992 dissertation is wrong. - _Sean A. Irvine_, Nov 21 2015
%F A004129 At present no g.f. is known. - _N. J. A. Sloane_, Jan 14 2021
%F A004129 a(n) <= binomial(n, 2) with equality only up to 4; 0.25 <= a(n)/n^2 <= 0.5 according to Graham & Sloane (1980), eq. (1). - _M. F. Hasler_, Aug 04 2026
%e A004129 From _M. F. Hasler_, Aug 04 2026: (Start)
%e A004129 For n = 2, we must use the 2-set S = {0, 1} in order to write 1 as a sum of two elements, and then no other number can be written as sum of distinct elements of S, so a(2) = 1.
%e A004129 For n = 3, we must use the 3-set S = {0, 1, 2} to write all numbers from 1 through m = 3 as sum of two distinct elements of S.
%e A004129 For n = 4, the 4-set S = {0, 1, 2, 4} allows one to write all numbers from 1 through m = 6 as sum of two distinct elements of S, and no larger m is possible, hence a(4) = 6.
%e A004129 For n = 5, the greedy construction which consists in adding a(n-1)+1 = 7 to the optimal set for n-1 still gives the maximum m = 9 = a(5).
%e A004129 For n = 6, the greedy construction would yield S = {0, 1, 2, 4, 7, 10} which gives only m = 12, but S = {0, 1, 2, 3, 6, 10} gives m = 13 = a(6). (End)
%Y A004129 Cf. A001212, A006638, A046693.
